TI  - Performance evaluation of an avian antibody-based particle-enhanced turbidimetric 
      assay for free kappa and lambda light chains.
PG  - 426-435
LID - 10.1080/00365513.2026.2684028 [doi]
AB  - Quantification of serum free light chains (FLCs) is essential for the diagnosis 
      and monitoring of plasma cell disorders. This study evaluated the analytical 
      performance of a novel avian IgY-based particle-enhanced turbidimetric 
      immunoassay (PETIA) for measuring free kappa (kappa) and lambda (lambda) light chains in 
      serum and plasma. The assay was evaluated on the Cobas Pro c503 (Roche 
      Diagnostics, Basel, Switzerland) and Abbott Architect c4000 (Abbott Laboratories, 
      Abbott Park, IL) platforms using samples from patients with monoclonal components 
      and healthy donors. Performance characteristics included precision, limit of 
      quantification (LoQ), linearity, interference, measuring interval, reagent 
      stability, high-dose hook effect, method comparison with the Diazyme FLC assay, 
      and reference interval verification in 70 healthy individuals. Within-run 
      precision was acceptable at clinically relevant concentrations. Functional LoQs 
      (15% CV) were 1.94 mg/L for kappa and 3.74 mg/L for lambda. Linearity was demonstrated on 
      the Abbott platform, with no significant interference from haemolysis, lipaemia 
      or bilirubin. Reagents were stable for 38 days on-board, and no hook effect was 
      observed at very high FLC concentrations. Method comparison showed strong 
      correlation with the Diazyme assay (kappa: r = 0.953; lambda: r = 0.881) and substantial 
      agreement for kappa/lambda ratios (Cohen's kappa = 0.64). Verified reference intervals aligned 
      with established standards. The IgY-based PETIA provides precise, 
      interference-resistant measurement of free kappa and lambda light chains across a wide 
      dynamic range, supporting its suitability for routine clinical use.
