TI  - Fluralaner, an innovative ectoparasiticide for use in cattle: UFLC-MS/MS method 
      development and validation for its pharmacokinetic characterization.
LID - 263 [pii]
LID - 10.1007/s11259-026-11193-5 [doi]
AB  - Fluralaner (FLR) is an isoxazoline ectoparasiticide of increasing interest for 
      use in cattle due to growing resistance to conventional antiparasitic compounds. 
      Reliable analytical methods are required to support its pharmacokinetic 
      characterisation and future residue and formulation studies. This study aimed to 
      develop and validate an Ultra-Fast Liquid Chromatography-Tandem Mass Spectrometry 
      (UFLC-MS/MS) method for determining FLR in bovine plasma and to investigate its 
      pharmacokinetic behaviour following intravenous administration. Plasma samples 
      were processed by solid-phase extraction and analysed using UFLC-MS/MS under 
      optimised chromatographic and mass spectrometric conditions. The method was 
      validated according to regulatory guidelines, showing linear response within the 
      working range (10-200 ng/mL), limits of detection and quantification of 5 and 10 
      ng/mL, recovery rates of 95.4-114.5%, negligible matrix effects, and a 
      coefficient of variation </= 10% for accuracy and precision. Stability under frozen 
      storage was confirmed. Method applicability was demonstrated in a pharmacokinetic 
      study conducted in four calves following intravenous administration of FLR 
      (2.5 mg/kg). Plasma concentrations remained quantifiable up to 28 days 
      post-administration, with detectable trace levels (LOD </= C </= LOQ) persisting 
      until day 42. FLR showed high initial plasma concentrations (C(0): 1162.9 ng/mL), 
      prolonged systemic exposure (AUC: 4400.5 +/- 819.6 ng.day/mL), extensive tissue 
      distribution (Vss: 3.7 +/- 0.8 L/kg), and a long terminal elimination half-life 
      (t(1)/(2)beta: 6.1 +/- 0.5 days). Overall, these results confirm the suitability of the 
      validated UFLC-MS/MS method for pharmacokinetic applications and provide the 
      first intravenous pharmacokinetic characterisation of FLR in cattle, establishing 
      a baseline for future studies exploring its behaviour following administration by 
      other therapeutic routes.
