TI  - A fiber optic particle plasmon resonance assay for the detection of neurofilament 
      light as a potential early biomarker for diabetic neuropathy.
PG  - 120697
LID - S0009-8981(25)00576-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.cca.2025.120697 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Emerging evidence suggests that neurofilament light (NfL) can serve 
      as a blood-based biomarker for diabetic neuropathy (DN), a severe complication 
      that affects half of patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM). Fiber optic 
      particle plasmon resonance (FOPPR) technology, which integrates optical 
      waveguides with noble metal nanoparticles, offers highly sensitive detection of 
      target analytes. It can potentially be applied to measure NfL in blood samples. 
      METHODS: A FOPPR-based plasma NfL assay was developed and validated for 
      imprecision, accuracy, limit of quantitation (LOQ), and analytical measurement 
      range (AMR). A comparison was made between this assay and a single molecule array 
      (Simoa) assay. RESULTS: Plasma NfL values were measured for 25 diabetic patients 
      with DN (T2DM with DN) and 19 diabetic patients without DN (T2DM). It was 
      observed that the plasma NfL values were significantly elevated in the T2DM with 
      DN group in comparison to the T2DM group. CONCLUSIONS: A plasma NfL assay has 
      been successfully developed based on the FOPPR technology. The assay offers a 
      promising approach for early clinical diagnosis of DN.
