TI  - Acrylamide in vegetable snacks by LC-MS/MS: in-house analytical method validation 
      and application to commercial samples from Brazil.
PG  - 1648-1657
LID - 10.1080/19440049.2025.2576661 [doi]
AB  - Acrylamide is a carcinogenic, genotoxic, and neurotoxic compound, which is formed 
      in thermally processed carbohydrate-rich food such as potato snacks, representing 
      a significant public health concern. Considering the growing consumption of 
      vegetable snacks as a healthier alternative to traditional potato chips and 
      crisps, it becomes relevant to assess their safety aspects associated with the 
      presence of this contaminant. Therefore, the aim of this study was to analyse and 
      compare the levels of acrylamide in commercial products made with different 
      vegetable matrices available in the Brazilian market. For that, an analytical 
      method using LC-MS/MS preceded by a modified QuEChERS sample preparation was 
      in-house validated. The limit of quantitation (LOQ) and limit of detection (LOD) 
      were 20 and 10 microg/kg, respectively. The linearity was evaluated in a range from 
      20 to 200 microg/kg showing a correlation coefficient (r) greater than 0.99. Overall, 
      coefficients of variation (CV%) for precision were below 20% and accuracy 
      (determined as recovery) ranged between 90 and 95%, at three fortification levels 
      (20 microg/kg, 80 microg/kg, and 200 microg/kg). A total of 33 types of vegetable snacks 
      acquired in the Brazilian market was analysed for the presence of acrylamide. 
      Considering the positive samples (above the LOQ, n = 32), the levels ranged from 
      25 to 11,381 mug/kg. Sweet potato-based and carrot-based snacks exhibited the 
      highest acrylamide concentrations. Only one sample (steamed cassava chips) showed 
      values < LOQ. This study shows the importance of evaluating the occurrence of 
      acrylamide in foods that are not traditionally monitored, but whose supply and 
      availability has been increasing in recent years in the market.
