TI  - 6-benzylaminopurine highly sensitive analysis by SPR sensor with bifunctional 
      magnetic molecularly imprinted polymers nanoparticles.
PG  - 140083
LID - S0308-8146(24)01733-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.foodchem.2024.140083 [doi]
AB  - A highly sensitive Surface Plasmon Resonance (SPR) sensor coupled magnetic 
      molecularly imprinted polymers nanoparticles (MMIPs NPs) was developed and 
      validated for the determination of 6-benzylaminopurine (6-BA) in vegetables. 
      MMIPs NPs were synthesized using methacrylic acid (MAA) and sodium p-styrene 
      sulfonate (SSS) as functional monomers. The SPR exhibited a linear dependence on 
      6-BA concentration in the range 5-300 pg/mL with a low limit of detection 
      (3.02 pg/mL) and limit of quantitation (10.08 pg/mL). The SPR signal of 
      6-BA-captured MAA/SSS-MMIPs NPs is higher than those of the structural analogues 
      (6-KT and 2-IP: 1.72 and 2.12 times) and the non-structural analogues (2, 4-D and 
      NAA: 2.31 and 2.57 times), indicating the SPR sensor has good selectivity for 
      6-BA. The recovery of the established method was between 93.8% and 108.6% with a 
      coefficient of variation less than 9.2% in four vegetables. This SPR sensor shows 
      great potential in detecting 6-BA in more vegetables.
